/* planning - how to keep a plan file: a checklist of steps you write to your plan path and update as you work */ # Planning Your plan file tracks tool work. On long tasks old messages get compacted away, but the plan file survives — and its contents are shown again at the end of every prompt, so it is how you remember what you were doing. ## When - A reminder will tell you when a task is running long. That is when you write the plan. - You can also start a plan yourself any time you notice a task has many steps. - A pure chat reply that uses no tools never needs a plan. ## The loop 1. Find your plan path in the CURRENT PLAN section of your system prompt. Copy it character for character. Never retype it from memory. 2. Write the checklist to that path: one action per line, each line starting with [ ]. Steps are actions ("write notes/a.txt"), not descriptions ("handle the files"). The last step is always: report the result to the user. 3. After each completed step, rewrite the whole file with [x] on finished lines. Always a full rewrite, never a partial edit. Never rewrite the plan right after a plan update itself — only after real task steps. 4. When a tool returns an error, rewrite the list with the failed line marked ! and the failed step broken into smaller steps under it. Then continue. An error string is a step to handle, not a reason to stop. 5. When a reminder asks about your plan, read the plan file and rewrite it so it matches what has actually happened. Then continue. 6. When every line is [x], stop updating the file and give your final answer. Do not delete the file — the next task overwrites it. For every tool call, follow the exact INPUT format in the AVAILABLE TOOLS section of your system prompt. ## Example Mid-task, a reminder tells you to plan. The task is: create notes/a.txt containing apples, read it back, say the word. You write to the plan path: [ ] write notes/a.txt containing apples [ ] read notes/a.txt back [ ] report the result to the user After the file is written, rewrite the plan: [x] write notes/a.txt containing apples [ ] read notes/a.txt back [ ] report the result to the user After the read, rewrite again with every line [x], then answer: the file contains the word apples. ## Gotchas - The plan path contains your agent id — copy it, never type it. - Writing replaces the whole file. That is exactly what plan updates rely on. - A pure chat reply that uses no tools needs no plan. 